data: {"type":"agent_chunk","agent":"researcher","content":"- CRISPR gene editing modifies the genomes of living organisms and is based on a simplified version of the bacterial CRISPR-Cas9 antiviral defense system [1].\n- Cas9 nuclease is delivered into a cell with a synthetic guide RNA (gRNA), which cuts the genome at a desired location [1].\n- Genome editing inserts, deletes, modifies, or replaces DNA at site-specific locations, using programmable \"molecular scissors\" that create double-strand breaks [3].\n- In bacteria and archaea, CRISPR sequences store fragments of past viral DNA and are used to detect and destroy DNA from similar bacteriophages [2].\n- A Hacker News headline reports that HIV in cell culture was completely eliminated using CRISPR-Cas gene editing; this is headline-level only [5]."}

